        <div class="tip-box">
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
          <p><strong>Measuring Recommendation:</strong> Always measure feet in the late afternoon. As you stand and walk during the day, feet expand slightly due to blood flow. Measuring in the evening ensures you never buy shoes that end up too small.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Comprehensive Foot Length Guide (500+ words) -->
    <section class="content-band">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <article class="prose-card">
          <header class="prose-header">
            <p class="mono-eyebrow">Foot Length Sizing Guide</p>
            <h2 class="prose-main-title">Why Foot Length is the Only True Sizing Standard</h2>
            <p class="prose-lead">Shoe size numbers are arbitrary regional conventions. Only your actual foot length in centimeters or millimeters provides a reliable, universal fit standard across every brand.</p>
          </header>

          <div class="content-prose">
            <p>Shoe size numbers are arbitrary regional conventions. In the United Kingdom, sizes count in 1/3-inch increments starting from a historic child size zero. The United States uses that same increment but starts at a different base point and adds separate offsets for men and women. European manufacturers use Paris Points (2/3 of a centimeter per size). Because of these competing systems, a person who wears a US Men's 9 might be a UK 8.5, an EU 42, or a Japanese 27. Physical foot measurement in centimeters or millimeters eliminates confusion when switching brands or ordering from overseas retailers.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">How Foot Length Relates to Shoe Interior (Lasting Allowance)</h3>
            <p>A frequent mistake when converting foot length to shoe size is expecting the shoe's internal cavity to match your foot measurement exactly. In shoemaking, shoes are built around wooden or plastic molds known as lasts. A well-fitted shoe must include an extra <strong>10 to 15 millimeters (approx. 0.4 to 0.6 inches)</strong> of clearance beyond your longest toe.</p>
            
            <div class="prose-callout">
              <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
              <p><strong>Toe Room Rule:</strong> Always ensure you have roughly one thumbnail's width (12–15 mm) between your longest toe and the end of the shoe when standing upright. This toe spring room prevents blisters and blackened toenails.</p>
            </div>

            <p>This clearance, known as the lasting allowance or toe spring room, allows your foot to elongate naturally as your arch flattens during weight-bearing strides. Without this buffer, your toes strike the front bumper with every downhill step, causing numbness, bunion irritation, and bruised toenails.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">Women's Shoe Size by Foot Length: Important Fit Nuances</h3>
            <p>While an adult woman and an adult man might both measure exactly 24.5 cm from heel to toe, their footwear needs rarely match. Female feet generally display a higher ball-to-heel width ratio — meaning the heel is noticeably narrower in proportion to the forefoot. When women buy men's or unisex shoes based purely on foot length, they frequently experience irritating heel slip and ankle blister points even when the toe length is correct. If you are a woman buying unisex athletic models, consider adding heel-grip pads or selecting brands that construct women's models on gender-specific lasts.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">Tracking Kids and Child Foot Length as They Grow</h3>
            <p>Children's feet develop rapidly, and their bones are soft and pliable, meaning kids often cannot feel or articulate when their shoes have become too small. Podiatrists recommend measuring children's foot length on a regular schedule:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Ages 1 to 3 (Toddlers):</strong> Feet grow approximately 1.5 millimeters per month. Re-measure every 2 to 3 months — toddlers frequently jump a full shoe size every 4 to 6 months.</li>
              <li><strong>Ages 4 to 8 (Young Children):</strong> Growth slows to roughly 1 millimeter per month. Measure foot length every 4 months.</li>
              <li><strong>Ages 9 and Up (Youth):</strong> Foot growth occurs in distinct spurts alongside height changes. Measure every 6 months.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>While it is tempting to buy children's shoes two sizes too large to "grow into," excessive length causes the child's foot to bend behind the shoe's engineered flex groove, leading to scuffing, tripping, and unnatural gait alterations. Keep extra toe room to roughly 12 millimeters (~0.5 inch).</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">How to Handle Uneven Foot Measurements</h3>
            <p>More than 60% of people have one foot that measures slightly longer or wider than the other — often by 3 to 6 millimeters (up to half a shoe size). When choosing footwear, always fit for your larger foot. You can easily adjust the fit for your smaller foot by adding a thin, tapered insole, using a tongue pad, or lacing with a runner's lock technique to prevent your heel from lifting.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ Accordion -->
    <section class="faq-band" id="faq">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <p class="mono-eyebrow">Help &amp; Guidelines</p>
        <h2 class="heading-md">Frequently Asked Questions: Shoe Size by Foot Length</h2>
